A channel is a typed communication mechanism between goroutines.

ch := make(chan int)

ch <- 10       // send
x := <-ch      // receive
close(ch)      // close

Unbuffered channels
An unbuffered channel provides direct synchronization between sender and receiver.
The send blocks until a receiver is ready, and the receive blocks until a sender is ready.

Buffered channels
ch := make(chan int, 3)
- capacity is 3

- Receiver gets blocked if the channel buffer is empty and no sender can provide a value.
- Sender gets blocked if the channel buffer is full and no receiver frees up space.

Directional channels
- chan int (Bi-directional)
- chan<- int (Send only)
- <-chan int (Receive only)
- Go channels are bidirectional by default,
  but we can use directional channel types—chan<- for send-only and <-chan for receive-only—to clearly define producer and consumer responsibilities.

Closing channels
 - close(ch)
 - when you try to read from a closed channel, it gives default values of that type
 - when you try to send to a closed channel, it throws panic
 - In buffered channel, when closed can be read untill buffer is empty
 - A receive from a closed channel does not block.
 - The sender should normally close the channel, not the receiver,
   Because the consumer doesn't necessarily know whether another sender still intends to send.
 - for value := range ch {
    fmt.Println(value)
    }
 - channel is closed
        +
   channel buffer is drained
        ↓
    range terminates
 - Closing a channel releases blocked receivers, but it does NOT safely release blocked senders.

Nil Channel
 - var ch chan int gives a nil channel
 - nil channel blocks forever while sending to it or receiving from it
 - select disables nil channel condition
 - panic happens when we try to close a nil channel

Select
select {
case value := <-ch1:
    fmt.Println(value)

case value := <-ch2:
    fmt.Println(value)
}
- The select waits until one of the communication operations can proceed.
- If both are ready, Go chooses randomly among the ready cases.

select {
case value := <-ch:
    fmt.Println(value)

default:
    fmt.Println("nothing available")
}
 - default statement here ensures that it is non-blocking

Program
done := make(chan struct{})

go func() {
    doWork()
    close(done)
}()

<-done
 - A technique used as signal that certain work is done

Deadlock
 - Deadlock because the send happens before a receiver exists.

ch := make(chan int)
ch <- 10
fmt.Println(<-ch)

error:
fatal error: all goroutines are asleep - deadlock!

Deadlock involving buffered channels:
ch := make(chan int, 2)
ch <- 1
ch <- 2
ch <- 3

 - Third send blocks because: there is no receiver and ccapacity is 2

